    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\alpha+\\beta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+2k\\pi$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $\\cos\\alpha>0$.\n\nEquate the sine coefficient to zero. The sum family gives $R=2\\cos\\alpha>0$; the other family, $\\alpha-\\beta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+2k\\pi$, gives $R=0$ and is excluded.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\cos(\\theta+\\alpha)+\\sin(\\theta+\\beta)&=(\\cos\\alpha+\\sin\\beta)\\cos\\theta+(\\cos\\beta-\\sin\\alpha)\\sin\\theta.\\end{align*}$$\nWhen $\\varphi=0$, coefficient matching gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\beta-\\sin\\alpha&=0\\\\R&=\\cos\\alpha+\\sin\\beta>0.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing $\\cos\\beta=\\sin(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\beta)$, the first equation gives either\n$$\\alpha+\\beta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+2k\\pi$$\nor\n$$\\alpha-\\beta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+2k\\pi,\\qquad k\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nFor the first family, $\\sin\\beta=\\cos\\alpha$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}R&=2\\cos\\alpha\\\\\\cos\\alpha&>0.\\end{align*}$$\nFor the second family, $\\sin\\beta=-\\cos\\alpha$, so $R=0$, which is excluded.\n\nTherefore all combinations are $\\alpha+\\beta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+2k\\pi$, with $k\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $\\cos\\alpha>0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 110
